{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## The defect\n\n2026-09-04, ~21:28-22:09 UTC: the Gerrit host stopped serving entirely for 41 minutes. Root cause\nCONFIRMED in the comments (full evidence, measurements and sandbox-fidelity caveats there).\n\n**The mechanism.** `infra/scripts/observability.sh` re-scans the ENTIRE retained journal on every\nrun with no `--since`, no `--lines` and no `timeout`: four fixed scans (`:760`, `:788`, `:816`,\n`:976`) plus one inside `publish_autodeploy_marker_delta` (`:883`) that runs once per marker call\nsite — 13 whole-journal scans per run. Measured under gp3-throttled conditions: the 300 s timer\nperiod is exhausted at ~1.37 GB of retained journal; the host's real journal is 1.7 GB (measured\nindependently by epic 6202 S3). Control: same journal, unbounded 33,936 ms vs `-n 5000` 798 ms —\n42.5x. The journald field index does not reduce bytes read.\n\n**The latch.** `infra/scripts/install-observability.sh:20-27` declares `Type=oneshot` with no\n`TimeoutStartSec`, so systemd reports `TimeoutStartUSec=infinity`; `:31` sets `OnUnitActiveSec=5min`,\ncomputed from the last COMPLETED activation. Confirmed on real systemd 255: a hung run does not\ndelay the timer, it DELETES its next elapse (`NextElapseUSecMonotonic=infinity`). One overrun\nsilences the probe permanently.\n\n**Why it is a defect, not a tuning miss.** `observability.sh:288-292` states the rule and bounds its\nown docker calls by it — *\"a wedged daemon under disk pressure must not hold the 5-minute timer\nopen\"*. The journal scans violate that rule in the same file. And the probe's contract at `:280` /\n`:487` is that a probe which cannot measure publishes NOTHING, and that its ABSENCE means the host\nis dead — a probe that can silence itself by hanging inverts its own heartbeat semantics.\n\n**Attribution** (evidence in the attribution comment): `786b-190d-5e3a-4194` created the latch\n(commit `6cd6f4e1f7c0`); `34cd-1a7c-b9b1-4b05` created the scan cost by putting a whole-journal scan\ninside a reusable per-marker helper (commit `e9d4d390c364`).\n\n## Constraints this work must respect\n\n- Terraform may be written and validated, never applied. The live host is not touched: no SSH, no\n  SSM. It is serving after a 41-minute outage.\n- Epic 6202 story S3 is concurrently editing `observability.sh`. Keep the diff tight.\n- The published metric contract must not change: these counters feed CloudWatch alarms whose\n  dimensions and names are fixed by `monitoring*.tf`.\n\n## Repair\n\n### R1 — make the scans bounded by changing how the cumulative count is maintained\n\nThe delta design persists a cumulative COUNT and derives the interval delta as `total - prev`. A\ncumulative total recomputed by scanning is why re-reading everything is structural. Two wrong\nrepairs are rejected up front:\n\n- `--since` alone turns `total` into a WINDOW count, so `total - prev` becomes a garbage delta.\n- A `timeout` alone yields a SILENTLY TRUNCATED count, and `total - prev` then publishes a plausible\n  wrong number. The script's own contract says a probe that cannot measure publishes nothing.\n\nSo the total is no longer recomputed; it is MAINTAINED. Each counter gains a persisted journald\ncursor and its scan reads only entries after it (`--after-cursor` / `--show-cursor`, journald's own\nexclusive primitive), adding the new matches to the persisted total. The published delta and every\ndocumented behaviour (cold-start seeding, lost-history suppression) are preserved; only the read\nvolume changes, from the whole retention to one interval.\n\nThe scan is additionally wall-clock bounded. On timeout, read failure, or an unusable cursor, the\ncursor is NOT advanced and NOTHING is published for that metric.\n\n### R2 — a finite start timeout, and I/O deprioritisation\n\n`install-observability.sh` renders `TimeoutStartSec` strictly less than the timer period, so a hung\nrun is killed before the next elapse. With a finite start timeout the activation always completes,\nso `OnUnitActiveSec=5min` re-arms by itself; the latch is removed at its root rather than papered\nover with a second trigger. The unit also gains `Nice=10` and `IOSchedulingClass=idle`, matching\n`rebar-autodeploy.service` — the probe is the heavier I/O consumer and currently competes with\nGerrit at full priority.\n\n### R3 — the same latch on `rebar-autodeploy.service`\n\nThat unit is also `Type=oneshot` with no `TimeoutStartSec`, driven by `OnUnitActiveSec=2min`, and\n`autodeploy.sh` makes unbounded `git clone` / `git fetch` calls. Identical failure mode, one line.\n\n### R4 — sibling bounds inside the same probe\n\nThe IMDS token/region/instance-id curls, the `git ls-remote` mirror comparison (whose sibling Gerrit\nREST read at the adjacent line correctly uses `--max-time 10`), and the `du` over unexpected\ndata-disk entries all run unbounded in the same 5-minute probe. Each is bounded.\n\n### NOT done here: capping journald\n\nDeliberately excluded. Epic 6202 S3 measured the journal at 1.7 GB against an implicit 4 GiB\n`SystemMaxUse` ceiling that was never exceeded, and already owns adding an explicit cap. A cap would\nnot have prevented this: 3 GiB still leaves 3 GiB to scan thirteen times. Recording a journald cap\nas the fix for this defect would be wrong, and `user_data.sh` only executes at instance launch so it\ncould not repair the running host in any case.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1 - Journal read volume per probe run does not grow with the size of the retained journal.\n      Verified by running the real script against two synthetic journals of very different sizes\n      with an argument-aware `journalctl` stub that records what it was asked to emit, and asserting\n      the volume read does not scale with corpus size. Behavioural, not a source inspection.\n- [ ] AC2 - The published delta stays correct under bounding: markers arriving during an interval\n      are counted exactly once, and the following run counts only what arrived since.\n- [ ] AC3 - When a journal scan fails or is cut off, the probe publishes NOTHING for that metric and\n      does not advance its cursor. No fabricated 0, no truncated delta.\n- [ ] AC4 - The rendered observability service carries a finite start timeout strictly less than its\n      timer period, asserted as a relation between the two rendered units rather than a literal.\n- [ ] AC5 - `rebar-autodeploy.service` carries a finite start timeout, for the same reason.\n- [ ] AC6 - The existing observability test suite still passes, demonstrating that the published\n      metric contract (names, values, cold-start seeding, rotation suppression, record anchoring)\n      is unchanged by the new state representation.\n- [ ] AC7 - Recorded in the tree at the site: bounding disk SPACE does not bound disk IOPS, and epic\n      6202 does not address this failure mode.\n\n## Out of scope\n\nDetection and admission control — a root-volume IOPS alarm, memory-pressure alarming, and\nheterogeneous gate admission. This change removes the confirmed cause; detection is tracked\nseparately so a detector is not built in place of a fix. `witted-invisible-roan` owns the memory\nhalf."}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788564753539744973,"uuid":"1604e572-f904-4c6b-baab-2b1bcddcfb75"}